  "query_preview": "..\n\nThursday night into Friday: Strong zonal flow sets up over the \nInland Northwest through this period as a warm front lifts into \nthe area. Friday looks mostly cloudy with a combination of \npatchy low stratus and widespread mid and high level clouds. \nPrecipitation chances move into the Cascades Friday morning, \nthen expand across the western Columbia Basin by afternoon. The \nwarm front continues to lift north into Friday night, shifting \nprecipitation chances over Northeast WA and North ID.\n\nSnow levels start out around 4-4.5 kft Friday morning, then rise\nto 7-9 kft by Friday evening as the warm, moist airmass takes\nover. Consequently, precipitation in the Cascades will begin as\nvalley rain and mountain snow in the morning and transition to\nall rain over the course of the day. \n\nSaturday: Saturday will feature a brief break from cloud cover \nand precipitation while we are in between systems. Highs will\nbe quite warm Saturday, ranging from the mid 50s to mid 60s. If\nyou're hoping to make any outdoor plans this weekend, Saturday\nis the day to do it.\n\nSaturday night through Wednesday: A progressive pattern persists \nwith a series of systems impacting the Inland Northwest into\nearly next week. The strongest of these systems arrives \nSaturday night into Sunday, bringing gusty winds in addition to\nrenewed precipitation chances. \n\nPrecipitation chances with this system increase for the \nCascades Saturday night and spread toward the eastern third of \nWA and the ID Panhandle by Sunday morning. Strong west-southwest\nflow will result in a pronounced rain shadow over much of\nthe Columbia Basin and North-Central WA. Snow levels will start\nout around 7.5 kft Saturday night, but will lower to around 4.5\nkft by Sunday morning, bringing potential for light snow over\nWashington Pass. Currently the NBM gives Washington Pass a 60\npercent chance of receiving at least 2 inches of snow by Sunday\nmorning. For 3 inches or more, chances drop to 10 percent.\n\nAs far as winds go, wind speeds and gusts will start to ramp up\novernight Saturday and are forecast to peak Sunday morning and\nearly afternoon. The strongest winds will be across the \nColumbia Basin, up the Okanogan Valley, and over the Waterville \nPlateau. Expect sustained wind speeds of 15-25 mph and gusts up\nto 40 mph for the aforementioned areas. /Fewkes",
